@meta
id: 24-03
title: Bubble a line up or down
chapter: 24
type: lesson
skills: ex-move
par: 21
minutes: 4
vscode: Alt-Up, Alt-Down
open: RELEASE.md

@brief
Two steps in the release checklist are swapped with their neighbours.
"Tag the release" on line 5 has to happen before "Push the tag" on
line 4. And "Deploy production" on line 7 has to wait until after the
staging smoke test on line 8.

Last lesson the destination was an absolute line. It can also be
relative to where you are. `:m-2` moves the current line to below the
line two above it, which is to say one line up. `:m+1` moves it to
below the next line, which is one line down. With the cursor on the
line, that is the whole command.

Put the cursor on each misplaced line and nudge it. Do not count line
numbers for the destination this time.

@keys
| `:m-2` | move the current line up one |
| `:m+1` | move the current line down one |
| `5G` | go to line 5 |

@hints
Move to the line first, then the command has no range and no absolute
target. Just `m` and an offset.
--
Up by one is `-2`, not `-1`, because the destination is the line the
text lands *after*. Landing after the line above you leaves you where
you were. Landing after the line two above you moves you up by one.
--
`5G` then `:m-2`. `7G` then `:m+1`. `:wq`.

@solution
    5G :m-2<Enter>  7G :m+1<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Eighteen keystrokes for two nudges.

`:m-2` is the one worth keeping. It reads oddly for about a day and
then it is the move you reach for whenever a line is one slot too low:
an import, a case in a switch, a bullet in a list. It does not need a
line number, so it works the same on line 5 and line 500, and a command
that does not need a number is a command you can repeat without
thinking. Lesson 16 makes that repeatable with one key.

VS Code has this as Alt-Up and Alt-Down, and honestly that is fine for
one line. What it does not have is `:5,9m-2`, which bubbles a whole
block up by one, or `:m$`, or `:m'a`. Same command, wider reach.

@start RELEASE.md
# Release checklist

- Bump the version in package.json
- Push the tag
- Tag the release
- Deploy staging
- Deploy production
- Smoke test the checkout flow on staging
- Announce in #releases

@expected RELEASE.md
# Release checklist

- Bump the version in package.json
- Tag the release
- Push the tag
- Deploy staging
- Smoke test the checkout flow on staging
- Deploy production
- Announce in #releases
